<div id="consent">
  {{{card}}}
  <p id="asks">Wants to act as you on chuggy</p>
  <script>
    for (const box of document.querySelectorAll('#consent input[name="grant_scope"]'))
      box.checked = true;
    document.querySelector("#consent form").prepend(document.getElementById("asks"));
  </script>
</div>
{{! The image's own consent.hbs, changed in what its card asks of a person. The
    image draws a tick box for each permission a client asked for, unticked,
    and its Allow grants the ticked ones. Pressed alone it grants none: the
    sign-in goes through, and the client is given no renewal token though it
    asked for one. This card is the client's name, the line above, Deny and
    Allow, and its Allow grants every permission asked. The element and the
    script above do that with the rules below, each under its reason.

    A PROGRAM THAT SIGNS IN FROM A TERMINAL IS SHOWN THIS PAGE, where
    `skip_consent` on its client or TRUSTED_CLIENT_IDS on this app would answer
    for the person. It is a public client with a loopback redirect, and any
    program on the same machine can ask as that client. This page is where a
    person sees that one is about to act as them.

    A SCRIPT, because the image's route hands a view the card as a string
    already drawn: nothing here can write a tick into it, a box is posted only
    when ticked, and the form has no id for a field outside it to join. The
    script ticks every permission and then puts the line into the form. Hydra
    has already refused a permission the client is not registered for, and the
    image's route grants the same list itself for a client it skips this page
    for. `remember` stays unticked, so a person is asked each time. The script
    runs, and the style below is read, only because the image sends no content
    security policy with this page. Under one that forbids inline script, a
    person meets the image's card again, its boxes unticked.

    NOTHING IS HIDDEN UNLESS THE SCRIPT RAN, AND ONLY WHILE WHAT IT DID HOLDS.
    The rule that hides the image's rows asks for the line in the form, for a
    permission ticked there, and for every tick box to be as the script left
    it: `remember` unticked and each other one ticked. A browser that runs no
    script draws the image's card, boxes and all, and so does one in which a
    box has changed since: never a card whose Allow grants other than what it
    shows. A client that asks for no permission is drawn the image's card as
    well, because the rule cannot tell its card from one whose permissions it
    does not know how to read.

    THE IMAGE'S VIEW ALSO INCLUDES THE `scripts` PARTIAL, AND THIS ONE DOES
    NOT. The layout this page is drawn in includes it already, and the script
    it loads declares a constant, so loaded again it is an error in the
    browser's console. It serves a password field, and this card has none.

    READ AGAINST THE IMAGE
    sha256:46a7bac1ad0c67c692b37d0380613fd4618fd751afac2c5314aa8d3ae77ef1f0
    because the script and the rules read the card by the names its form posts
    and by a tick box being what it draws a permission as. Under one that drew
    a permission as anything else the script would tick nothing, the rule
    would find no permission ticked, and nothing would be hidden: a person
    would meet that image's own card, whole, under the line. What the view
    cannot do is know another image's card for one it reads rightly. One that
    kept the tick boxes and drew something new beside them could have it
    hidden, and one that held its rows otherwise could lose its buttons. So
    tests/ory-ui-view.py holds ory-ui.yaml to that image and to the path it
    reads this view from, and another is named here once Allow has been
    pressed under it and the token answer read for what was granted. }}
<style>
  {{! The line is drawn in the form alone, where the script puts it, and
      close under the name: the card keeps its heading and its form further
      apart than a name and what is said of it. }}
  #consent > #asks {
    display: none;
  }
  #asks {
    margin: -1.5rem 0 2rem;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-muted);
  }

  {{! Everything the form holds but the row Deny and Allow are in, and the
      row of links to a client's own policy and terms, which the image draws
      for a client registered with them. Hidden only while the form holds the
      line and a permission that is ticked, and every tick box in it is as the
      script left it: `remember` unticked, each other one ticked. Without the
      term for a ticked permission, a card that drew its permissions as
      anything but tick boxes would be Deny and Allow alone, whatever that
      Allow then granted. The boxes and the buttons are read by the names the
      form posts them under, and the rows are taken to be the children of the
      one element the form holds them in. }}
  form:has(> #asks):has(input[name="grant_scope"]:checked):not(
      :has(
        input[type="checkbox"]:not(:checked, [name="remember"]),
        input[name="remember"]:checked
      )
    )
    > div
    > :not(:has(button[name="consent_action"], a)) {
    display: none;
  }

  {{! The image marks keyboard focus on Allow by the colour of a border it
      does not draw, and on Deny by a darker text alone. So a ring is drawn,
      inside the button because the card's grids clip what is drawn outside
      one. }}
  #consent button[name="consent_action"]:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid currentcolor;
    outline-offset: -5px;
  }
</style>
